with a 2x2 grid layout providing simultaneous views of:\\n1. **Pane 1 (top-left)**: CLI running Claude agent\\n2. **Pane 2 (top-right)**: Live-streaming activity logs\\n3. **Pane 3 (bottom-left)**: Vim opened to output directory for file browsing\\n4. **Pane 4 (bottom-right)**: Master task list showing completed, in-progress, and upcoming tasks\\n\\n## Requirements\\n\\n### Functional Requirements\\n\\n#### FR-1: Tmux Session Management\\n- FR-1.1: Script to create a new tmux session named \\"ensemble-monitor\\" (or configurable name)\\n- FR-1.2: Script to attach to existing session if it already exists\\n- FR-1.3: Script to cleanly kill/destroy the monitoring session\\n- FR-1.4: Session should persist if terminal disconnects\\n\\n#### FR-2: Pane 1 - Claude CLI\\n- FR-2.1: Run the ensemble CLI with Claude in interactive mode\\n- FR-2.2: Position in top-left quadrant\\n- FR-2.3: Allow user interaction with the CLI\\n- FR-2.4: Support different ensemble entry points (Executive Director, etc.)\\n\\n#### FR-3: Pane 2 - Activity Logs\\n- FR-3.1: Stream logs/backend.log in real-time (tail -f or similar)\\n- FR-3.2: Position in top-right quadrant\\n- FR-3.3: Show colored output if logs support ANSI colors\\n- FR-3.4: Auto-scroll to latest entries\\n- FR-3.5: Consider alternative: parse activity tracker events for cleaner output\\n\\n#### FR-4: Pane 3 - Vim File Browser\\n- FR-4.1: Open vim with netrw or NERDTree at the output directory\\n- FR-4.2: Position in bottom-left quadrant\\n- FR-4.3: Allow user to navigate and view generated files\\n- FR-4.4: Support configurable output directory path\\n- FR-4.5: Default to ensemble_ui/output directory\\n\\n#### FR-5: Pane 4 - Master Task List\\n- FR-5.1: Display a continuously-updated list of all swarm tasks\\n- FR-5.2: Position in bottom-right quadrant\\n- FR-5.3: Tasks grouped by status:\\n  - \\u2705 Completed tasks\\n  - \\ud83d\\udd04 In-progress tasks\\n  - \\u23f3 Upcoming/pending tasks\\n- FR-5.4: Each task shown as short topic (<10 words)\\n- FR-5.5: Auto-refresh as swarm updates task states\\n- FR-5.6: Source task data from:\\n  - ~/.ensemble/projects/{project_id}/project.json\\n  - Activity tracker events\\n  - Project tracking system\\n\\n#### FR-6: Task List Update Mechanism\\n- FR-6.1: Create a task list watcher script that monitors project state\\n- FR-6.2: Update display every 1-2 seconds (configurable)\\n- FR-6.3: Tasks updated by swarm agents via project_tracking tool\\n- FR-6.4: Show task count summary at top (e.g., \\"3 done | 2 active | 5 pending\\")\\n\\n### Non-Functional Requirements\\n\\n#### NFR-1: Usability\\n- NFR-1.1: Single command to launch the entire dashboard\\n- NFR-1.2: Clear documentation on how to navigate between panes (Ctrl-b + arrow keys)\\n- NFR-1.3: Reasonable default pane sizes (equal 50/50 splits)\\n\\n#### NFR-2: Compatibility\\n- NFR-2.1: Work with tmux 3.0+ (modern features like -Z for zoom)\\n- NFR-2.2: Work on macOS and Linux\\n- NFR-2.3: Support both bash and zsh shells\\n\\n#### NFR-3: Configurability\\n- NFR-3.1: Allow customization of session name\\n- NFR-3.2: Allow customization of output directory\\n- NFR-3.3: Allow customization of log file path\\n- NFR-3.4: Allow customization of project ID for task tracking\\n\\n## Technical Architecture\\n\\n### Components\\n\\n1. **start_monitor.sh** - Main script to launch tmux dashboard\\n   - Creates tmux session\\n   - Sets up 2x2 layout\\n   - Launches appropriate commands in each pane\\n\\n2. **stop_monitor.sh** - Script to cleanly stop the dashboard\\n   - Kills tmux session\\n   - Cleans up any background processes\\n\\n3. **task_watcher.py** (or .sh) - Script for Pane 4\\n   - Watches ~/.ensemble/projects/ for changes\\n   - Parses project.json for task status\\n   - Formats and displays task list\\n   - Refreshes on interval or file change (inotify/fswatch)\\n\\n### Integration Points\\n\\n1. **Project Tracking System** (existing)\\n   - Location: ~/.ensemble/projects/{project_id}/project.json\\n   - Contains: tasks with status (todo, in_progress, completed, blocked)\\n   - Updated by: agents via project_tracking tool\\n\\n2. **Activity Tracker** (existing)\\n   - Location: src/runtime/agents/activity_tracker.py\\n   - Events: TASK_UPDATE, AGENT_STARTED, AGENT_COMPLETED\\n   - Could emit events to a log file for task_watcher to consume\\n\\n3. **Backend Logs** (existing)\\n   - Location: logs/backend.log\\n   - Contains: Agent execution logs, errors, status updates\\n\\n## User Interface Layout\\n\\n```\\n\\u250c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u252c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2510\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502                                     \\u2502\\n\\u2502     PANE 1: Claude CLI              \\u2502     PANE 2: Activity Logs           \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502                                     \\u2502\\n\\u2502     $ ensemble run --problem \\"...\\"  \\u2502     [12:34:56] Agent started: ED    \\u2502\\n\\u2502     > Thinking...                   \\u2502     [12:34:57] Tool: spawn_agent    \\u2502\\n\\u2502     > Created requirements.md       \\u2502     [12:35:01] Agent: DevManager    \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502     [12:35:02] Writing: arch.md     \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502                                     \\u2502\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u253c\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2524\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502                                     \\u2502\\n\\u2502     PANE 3: Vim File Browser        \\u2502     PANE 4: Task List               \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502                                     \\u2502\\n\\u2502     \\" Press ? for help              \\u2502     \\u2550\\u2550\\u2550 ENSEMBLE TASKS \\u2550\\u2550\\u2550          \\u2502\\n\\u2502     ..                              \\u2502     3 done | 2 active | 5 pending   \\u2502\\n\\u2502     requirements.md                 \\u2502                                     \\u2502\\n\\u2502     architecture.md                 \\u2502     \\u2705 Create requirements doc      \\u2502\\n\\u2502     milestones.md                   \\u2502     \\u2705 Design architecture          \\u2502\\n\\u2502     src/                            \\u2502     \\u2705 Break down milestones        \\u2502\\n\\u2502       main.py                       \\u2502     \\ud83d\\udd04 Implement backend API        \\u2502\\n\\u2502       tests/                        \\u2502     \\ud83d\\udd04 Write unit tests             \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502     \\u23f3 Implement frontend           \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502     \\u23f3 Integration tests            \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502     \\u23f3 Documentation                \\u2502\\n\\u2502                                     \\u2502                                     \\u2502\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2534\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2500\\u2518\\n```\\n\\n## Implementation Milestones\\n\\n### Milestone 1: Basic Tmux Layout (MVP)\\n- Create start_monitor.sh with 2x2 layout\\n- Pane 1: Simple shell (placeholder for CLI)\\n- Pane 2: tail -f on backend.log\\n- Pane 3: vim opened to output directory\\n- Pane 4: Simple watch command on project file\\n\\n**Deliverables:**\\n- scripts/deployment/start_monitor.sh\\n- scripts/deployment/stop_monitor.sh\\n- Documentation in README\\n\\n### Milestone 2: Task Watcher Script\\n- Create task_watcher.py to parse project.json\\n- Formatted output with status icons\\n- Auto-refresh on interval\\n- Summary counts\\n\\n**Deliverables:**\\n- scripts/monitoring/task_watcher.py\\n- Integration with tmux pane 4\\n\\n### Milestone 3: Full Integration\\n- Connect Claude CLI to Pane 1\\n- Enhanced log formatting for Pane 2\\n- Polish and documentation\\n\\n**Deliverables:**\\n- Updated start_monitor.sh with CLI integration\\n- User documentation\\n- Example usage in README\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n1. **One-command launch**: Run `./scripts/deployment/start_monitor.sh` and get full dashboard\\n2. **Task visibility**: See all tasks update in real-time as swarm operates\\n3. **File access**: Easily browse and view output files without leaving tmux\\n4. **Log streaming**: See activity logs scroll in real-time\\n5. **CLI access**: Interact with Claude CLI while monitoring\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n1. User has tmux installed (v3.0+)\\n2. User has vim installed\\n3. Project uses ~/.ensemble/projects/ for project tracking\\n4. Activity logs are written to logs/backend.log\\n5. User is familiar with basic tmux navigation (Ctrl-b + arrows)\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n\\n1. Custom tmux theme/styling (use user\'s existing tmux config)\\n2. Mouse support configuration (rely on user\'s tmux settings)\\n3. Multiple project monitoring (single project at a time)\\n4. Historical task analytics (just current state)\\n5. GUI-based monitoring (this is terminal-only)\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n### External\\n- tmux >= 3.0\\n- vim (with netrw built-in)\\n- Python 3.8+ (for task_watcher.py)\\n- bash or zsh\\n\\n### Internal\\n- Project tracking system (~/.ensemble/projects/)\\n- Activity tracker (src/runtime/agents/activity_tracker.py)\\n- Existing deployment scripts (scripts/deployment/)\\n\\n## Open Questions\\n\\n1. Should task_watcher use filesystem watching (fswatch/inotify) or polling?\\n   - **Decision**: Start with polling (simpler), optimize later if needed\\n\\n2. Should Pane 2 show raw logs or parsed activity events?\\n   - **Decision**: Start with raw logs, consider parsed view as enhancement\\n\\n3. How should the CLI pane be started - automatically run a command or wait for user?\\n   - **Decision**: Start with shell ready for user to run command, consider auto-start option\\n"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01HkynJAyr4M9GV7GzzZVMjr', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/tmux_monitoring_dashboard/architecture.md', 'content': "# Tmux Monitoring Dashboard - Architecture Proposal\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\n### Architectural Pattern\n**Monitoring Dashboard with Modular Components**\n- **Type**: Terminal-based Monitoring System\n- **Pattern**: Microservice-inspired modular design\n- **Core Philosophy**: Separation of Concerns with Lightweight Integration\n\n### High-Level Architecture\n1. **Session Management Layer**: Tmux Session Creation/Control\n2. **Monitoring Components**:\n   - CLI Interface Pane\n   - Logging Pane\n   - File Browser Pane\n   - Task Tracking Pane\n3. **Background Services**:\n   - Task Watcher\n   - Log Streamer\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n### Core Technologies\n- **Terminal Multiplexer**: Tmux (v3.0+)\n- **Scripting Languages**: \n  - Bash (Session Management)\n  - Python 3.8+ (Task Watching, Advanced Processing)\n- **Text Editor**: Vim (with netrw/NERDTree)\n\n### Supporting Tools\n- **File Watching**: \n  - `inotifywait` (Linux)\n  - `fswatch` (macOS)\n- **Log Processing**: \n  - `tail`\n  - Python's `watchdog` library (optional)\n\n## System Components\n\n### 1. start_monitor.sh\n**Responsibilities**:\n- Create tmux session\n- Configure 2x2 layout\n- Launch individual panes\n- Handle session persistence\n\n**Key Functions**:\n```bash\ncreate_tmux_session() {\n  tmux new-session -d -s ensemble-monitor\n  setup_panes\n  configure_pane_contents\n}\n```\n\n### 2. task_watcher.py\n**Responsibilities**:\n- Monitor project state file\n- Parse task statuses\n- Generate formatted task list\n- Refresh on interval\n\n**Key Methods**:\n```python\ndef watch_project_state():\n    # Watch ~/.ensemble/projects/{project_id}/project.json\n    while True:\n        update_task_list()\n        time.sleep(REFRESH_INTERVAL)\n\ndef update_task_list():\n    # Read JSON, categorize tasks\n    # Render to tmux pane\n```\n\n### 3. log_streamer.sh\n**Responsibilities**:\n- Stream backend logs\n- Handle log rotation\n- Provide real-time view\n\n**Key Functions**:\n```bash\nstream_logs() {\n  tail -f logs/backend.log | \\\n    colorize_output | \\\n    tmux load-buffer -\n}\n```\n\n## Data Flow\n\n```\n[Project State] \n    ↓\ntask_watcher.py \n    ↓\n[Formatted Task List]\n    ↓\nTmux Pane 4\n```\n\n```\n[Backend Logs] \n    ↓\nlog_streamer.sh \n    ↓\n[Colorized/Formatted Logs]\n    ↓\nTmux Pane 2\n```\n\n## Deployment Strategy\n\n### Installation\n1. Copy scripts to `scripts/deployment/`\n2. Ensure executable permissions\n3. Add to PATH or use full path\n\n### Execution\n```bash\n# Launch dashboard\n./start_monitor.sh\n\n# Stop dashboard\n./stop_monitor.sh\n```\n\n## Testing Strategy\n\n### Component Testing\n- Unit test each script independently\n- Mock tmux interactions\n- Validate output formatting\n\n### Integration Testing\n- Test full dashboard launch\n- Verify pane interactions\n- Check log streaming\n- Validate task list updates\n\n## Configuration\n\n### Environment Variables\n- `ENSEMBLE_PROJECT_ID`: Select project to monitor\n- `TMUX_LOG_PATH`: Custom log file location\n- `TASK_REFRESH_INTERVAL`: Update frequency\n\n## Risks and Mitigations\n\n### Risk: Performance Overhead\n**Mitigation**: \n- Use efficient polling intervals\n- Implement lightweight parsing\n- Consider compiled Python or Go for task watching\n\n### Risk: tmux Version Compatibility\n**Mitigation**:\n- Detect tmux version\n- Provide fallback configurations\n- Clear version requirements in documentation\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n### Alternative 1: Web-based Dashboard\n**Pros**: \n- Rich visualizations\n- Remote access\n\n**Cons**:\n- Increased complexity\n- Requires web server\n- Less terminal-native\n\n### Alternative 2: Separate Terminal Windows\n**Pros**: \n- Simple to implement\n- No tmux dependency\n\n**Cons**:\n- Context switching\n- Manual window management\n- Less integrated\n\n## Open Questions\n\n1. Log parsing strategy (raw vs. structured)\n2. Error handling for missing project files\n3. Customization options for different workflows\n\n## Roadmap\n\n### MVP (Milestone 1)\n- Basic tmux layout\n- Simple log streaming\n- Static task list\n\n### V2 \n- Dynamic task updates\n- Enhanced log parsing\n- Configurable views\n\n## Compliance Checklist\n✅ Single-command launch\n✅ Real-time task tracking\n✅ Log streaming\n✅ File browser integration\n✅ Supports macOS/Linux\n✅ Minimal external dependencies\n\n## Recommended Next Steps\n1. Implement start_monitor.sh\n2. Develop task_watcher.py\n3. Create comprehensive test suite\n4. Document usage and configuration"}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01HkynJAyr4M9GV7GzzZVMjr', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/tmux_monitoring_dashboard/architecture.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/tmux_monitoring_dashboard/architecture_20260116_224324.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/tmux_monitoring_dashboard/architecture_20260116_224324.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-3-5-haiku-20241022', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be s